From 31fb69ddca6fc085d085ce162b27fd7d591ed578 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: oslab2 <oslab2@oslab2>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 09:44:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] pre-gso: step 2 - burst-end detection (sleep flush)

Implements the paper aggregation_end "conclusion of a message burst"
without application changes. Previously (reference design) the app
signalled burst end with MSG_FINISH_SEND; now the kernel infers it:
a burst is over when the sending task voluntarily blocks.

- Per-task plug (include/net/tcp_pre_gso.h), modelled on the block
  layer plug: a task that stages Pre-GSO bytes records the socket
  (with a reference) in current->pre_gso_plug. Only the owning task
  adds/removes entries, so no lock is needed.
- sched_submit_work() flushes the plug when the task blocks. Involuntary
  preemption (softirq/ksoftirqd contention) does not reach this path,
  so aggregation is kept exactly where Pre-GSO targets.
- Flush runs without sleeping: bh_lock_sock + commit, or, if the socket
  is owned by a user context, TCP_PRE_GSO_FLUSH_DEFERRED handled in
  tcp_release_cb().
- fork() clears the inherited pointer; do_exit() flushes and frees the
  plug (the final schedule of a dying task skips sched_submit_work).
- If the plug cannot grow, the batch is committed immediately
  (commit_plug_overflow) so a batch can never stall.
- Timer and sleep paths share tcp_pre_gso_pending_committable(), which
  keeps the step-1 write-queue invariant.
- debugfs: commit_sleep and commit_plug_overflow counters.
- No new sysctl: sleep flush is part of aggregation_end and is always
  active when Pre-GSO is enabled. timeout_us keeps its meaning.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
